.TH KENTSCRIPT-SYSCALL 3 "KentScript Manual"
.SH NAME
kentscript-syscall \- KentScript system call functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B unsafe {
.br
let ret = syscall(60, 0);  :: exit(0)
.br
}
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions provide direct access to system calls. They must be called from within an \fBunsafe\fR block. System call numbers vary by platform.
.SH COMMON SYSTEM CALLS (Linux x86-64)
.TP
\fBsyscall(num: int, *args) -> int\fR
Make a raw system call by number with up to 6 arguments. Returns the result or negative error code.
.SS Process Management
.TP
60 - exit(status: int)\fR
Terminate the process with given status.
.TP
231 - exit_group(status: int)\fR
Exit all threads in the process.
.TP
57 - fork()\fR
Create a new process. Returns 0 to child, child PID to parent.
.TP
59 - execve(path: *char, argv: **char, envp: **char)\fR
Execute a program.
.TP
60 - wait4(pid: int, status: *int, options: int, rusage: *struct)\fR
Wait for process to change state.
.TP
62 - kill(pid: int, sig: int)\fR
Send signal to process.
.SS File Operations
.TP
2 - open(path: *char, flags: int, mode: int)\fR
Open a file. Returns file descriptor.
.TP
3 - close(fd: int)\fR
Close a file descriptor.
.TP
0 - read(fd: int, buf: *char, count: int)\fR
Read from file descriptor.
.TP
1 - write(fd: int, buf: *char, count: int)\fR
Write to file descriptor.
.TP
8 - lseek(fd: int, offset: int, whence: int)\fR
Reposition read/write file offset.
.SS Memory Management
.TP
9 - mmap(addr: *void, length: int, prot: int, flags: int, fd: int, offset: int)\fR
Map or unmap files or devices into memory.
.TP
11 - munmap(addr: *void, length: int)\fR
Unmap memory.
.TP
10 - mprotect(addr: *void, len: int, prot: int)\fR
Set memory protection.
.SS Threading
.TP
56 - clone(flags: int, stack: *void, parent_tid: *int, child_tid: *void, tls: *void)\fR
Create a new process/thread.
.TP
120 - set_tid_address(tid_ptr: *int)\fR
Set pointer to thread ID.
.SS Time
.TP
201 - clock_gettime(clock_id: int, timespec: *struct)\fR
Get current time.
.TP
231 - clock_nanosleep(clock_id: int, flags: int, req: timespec, rem: timespec)\fR
High-resolution sleep.
.SS Networking
.TP
41 - socket(domain: int, type: int, protocol: int)\fR
Create socket.
.TP
42 - bind(sockfd: int, addr: *sockaddr, addrlen: int)\fR
Bind socket to address.
.TP
43 - listen(sockfd: int, backlog: int)\fR
Listen for connections.
.TP
43 - accept(sockfd: int, addr: *sockaddr, addrlen: *int)\fR
Accept connection.
.TP
42 - connect(sockfd: int, addr: *sockaddr, addrlen: int)\fR
Connect to remote socket.
.SS Information
.TP
160 - uname(buf: *utsname)\fR
Get system information.
.TP
63 - getcwd(buf: *char, size: int)\fR
Get current working directory.
.TP
62 - getpid()\fR
Get process ID.
.TP
110 - getuid()\fR
Get real user ID.
.SH EXAMPLE
unsafe {
.br
    :: Write to stdout (fd=1)
    let msg = "Hello, world!\n";
    
    :: syscall number 1 = write
    syscall(1, 1, msg, 15);
    
    
    :: Exit with code 0 (syscall 60)
.br
    syscall(60, 0);
.br
}
.SH NOTES
System call numbers differ across architectures. This documentation is for Linux x86-64.
